Working on the Android stopwatch project offered our team an excellent opportunity to experience collaborative software development while applying formal design principles we’ve learned in practice.
We organized our workflow by dividing responsibilities across the user interface, model implementation, and state machine logic. The repository was central to our collaboration, allowing for smooth integration of
contributions. Regular testing ensured the stopwatch remained functional throughout development. A noteworthy aspect of our process was iterative refactoring: as we connected the state machine 
to the UI, we refined both the adapter and model facade to simplify event handling and enhance responsiveness without disrupting the underlying logic. Each modeled state — running, stopped, lap running, 
and lap stopped — corresponds directly to a class implementing the StopwatchState interface. Actions and transitions from the model, such as onStartStop and onLapReset, were faithfully translated into methods 
in the state machine. One difference is that the model diagrams abstracted away implementation details such as threading, tick scheduling, and integration with the Android lifecycle, all of which were necessary 
in code to ensure smooth UI updates. Additionally, the model did not include the adapter and facade components, which in practice facilitate communication between the user interface and the state machine.
Reflecting on the process, modeling first proved highly beneficial, providing a clear blueprint for expected behavior and allowing us to anticipate edge cases before coding. However, seeing the system in 
action highlighted additional considerations, like precise tick timing and lifecycle management, which led to minor adjustments in the implementation. If we were to revise the model now, we would incorporate 
these practical concerns to make it more aligned with real-world execution. Overall, the alignment between model and code, combined with iterative testing and careful refactoring, enabled us to deliver a reliable,
maintainable stopwatch while extending our understanding of how design and implementation interact in Android development.
